  "id": "Compound::DB01064 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Isoprenaline, a sympathomimetic agent, is known to bind to beta-adrenergic receptors, a class of G-protein-coupled receptors that mediate the effects of catecholamines like norepinephrine and epinephrine. The visible evidence suggests that Isoprenaline interacts with Adrb1, a receptor that participates in multiple biological processes, including norepinephrine and epinephrine binding. These interactions are supported by multiple paths, each indicating a direct link between Isoprenaline and Adrb2 through a chain of participation in receptor-mediated signaling pathways. The consistent binding of Isoprenaline to Adrb1, coupled with its participation in the binding of norepinephrine and epinephrine, implies a mechanistic relationship where Isoprenaline modulates Adrb2 through the activation of beta-adrenergic receptors. The evidence supports a direct, biologically plausible mechanism by which Isoprenaline influences Adrb2, aligning with the known role of beta-adrenergic receptors in cellular signaling.",
